+Partial reads initially verify the whole covering entry. From those verified bytes,
+readers remember checksums for requested ranges in memory, attached to the owning entry.
+Later reads use the smallest covering checksum, or share a whole-entry read already in
+progress, without copying payload bytes on disk. Direct I/O still rounds reads to 4-KiB
+alignment. These partial checksums have no disk records or separate payload ownership
+and are discarded on entry removal or restart. Distinct requested ranges add in-memory
+metadata until the entry is removed. Choosing a checksum scans these ranges under the
+shard's index lock, so both memory use and lookup work grow with distinct ranges.
